        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41257</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/41257"/>
        <updated>2025-09-24T12:32:23.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47647?u=2fbfc431a85d357f00c0146d5e55b425b7f00a3d&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/>

<h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">Chrome 136 introduces the CSS <a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color-hdr/#the-dynamic-range-limit-property" rel="nofollow"><code class="notranslate">dynamic-range-limit</code></a> property; see <a href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5146250411769856" rel="nofollow">https://chromestatus.com/feature/5146250411769856</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto">The <code class="notranslate">dynamic-range-limit-mix()</code> function is also supported, but I'm not sure which version of Chrome this first appeared in. I've asked Chrome engineering for an answer to this question.</p>
<p dir="auto">This PR documents both of the two features mentioned above.</p>
<div class="markdown-alert markdown-alert-note" dir="auto"><p class="markdown-alert-title" dir="auto"><svg class="octicon octicon-info mr-2" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 8a8 8 0 1 1 16 0A8 8 0 0 1 0 8Zm8-6.5a6.5 6.5 0 1 0 0 13 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 0-13ZM6.5 7.75A.75.75 0 0 1 7.25 7h1a.75.75 0 0 1 .75.75v2.75h.25a.75.75 0 0 1 0 1.5h-2a.75.75 0 0 1 0-1.5h.25v-2h-.25a.75.75 0 0 1-.75-.75ZM8 6a1 1 0 1 1 0-2 1 1 0 0 1 0 2Z"></path></svg>Note</p><p dir="auto">Some of the description in the property page might want to go into a separate glossary entry, but I wasn't sure how best to chop up this information. Opinions welcome.</p>
</div>

